<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # We All Do Chores. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping Around the House</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We All Do Chores.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Develop short poems or rhymes. Interactive: Summarize feedback from peer reviews.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a short poem or rhyme about household chores using simple vocabulary and repetition (e.g., "I sweep the floor, I wash the plate, I clean the room, I can't be late!"). Can write a simple summary of peer feedback on a classmate's poem or rhyme about chores (e.g., "Maria's rhyme was fun. She used good words and talked about cleaning the house."). Can organize peer feedback into simple categories (e.g., what they liked, "Good job: used rhyming words." and what can be improved, "Needs work: add more lines about chores.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or images of various household chores (e.g., "wash the dishes," "sweep the floor," "clean the room," "water the plants," "make the bed"). Students verbally identify the chores by saying the full phrase. The teacher asks, "What do you do at home?" and students share one chore they do. This activates prior vocabulary from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a very simple rhyming couplet on the board, like: "I help my mom, I sweep the floor. / I clean the room, and open the door." The teacher reads it aloud, emphasizing the rhyming words ("floor" and "door") and the chore verbs. The teacher highlights the /w/ sound in "wash" and the /s/ sound in "sweep" while reading other examples. The teacher also shows how to form simple present tense sentences: "I wash the dishes every day."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do words in a rhyme sound the same at the end? (e.g., 'floor' and 'door') Yes or no?" (Expected answer: Yes.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we write about things we do at home, like chores? Yes or no?" (Expected answer: Yes.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[5-7 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, clear rhyming poem about household chores written on a large chart or projected onto the board. The poem explicitly uses present simple tense, core vocabulary, and simple rhyming patterns suitable for A1.3. For example:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">"Every day, I clean my room,<br>With my little broom. <br>I wash the dishes, plate by plate,<br>I help my family, it's never too late!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Discussing routines: Describing daily tasks and house chores according to context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is this poem about?" (Expected answer: Chores/Helping at home.) "What two chores does the poem talk about?" (Expected answer: Cleaning the room and washing dishes.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> The teacher writes chore verbs and nouns separately on the board (e.g., "clean," "wash," "room," "dishes"). Students come up and draw lines to match the verb to the correct noun as it appears in the poem (e.g., clean - room, wash - dishes).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[8-10 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences about chores using present simple, vocabulary (dishes, broom, floor, clean, wash, sweep), and identifying simple rhyming words. Phonemic awareness of closed and open syllables (e.g., "cook," "do").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Fill-in-the-blanks:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with incomplete sentences that resemble lines from a poem about chores. Students fill in the blanks with appropriate chore verbs or nouns from a word bank. Example: "I ___ the floor with a ___." (sweep, broom). "I ___ the clothes ___ day." (fold, every). This reinforces present simple and target vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Rhyme Starters:</b> The teacher gives students sentence starters and one rhyming word, asking them to complete the line about a chore. Example: "My bedroom is tidy, I do it today. I like to ___ and ___." (e.g., "play," "stay"). Students complete 3-4 such lines, focusing on using chore verbs and nouns in the present simple tense. The teacher models a closed syllable word like "cook" and an open syllable word like "do," asking students to identify which is which when they use their own words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check & Feedback:</b> Students work in pairs to review their completed sentences from activity 2. They check if their partner used chore vocabulary correctly and if the sentences make sense. They practice asking, "What do you do at home?" and "How often do you clean?" using WH-questions and frequency adverbs (e.g., "every day," "often"). They note down one good sentence and one sentence that needs improvement from their partner's work. This prepares them for peer review in the next stage.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[15-18 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students write their own short, 4-line poem or rhyme about household chores, focusing on the scenario "Helping Around the House."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual written product (a short poem/rhyme).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reminds students of the example poem from Stage 2 and the completed sentences from Stage 3.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to write a 4-line poem about chores they do at home. They should try to use simple rhyming words and include at least two different chore verbs (e.g., wash, clean, sweep, tidy up, feed, make, water) and related nouns (e.g., dishes, floor, room, plant, bed). They must use the present simple tense (e.g., "I sweep the floor," "My mom helps me").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering support and encouragement, checking for use of target vocabulary and simple present tense. Pragmatic language use is encouraged by asking students, "What chores do you want to write about?" and "How do you help your family?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in writing, applying learned grammar (present simple, frequency adverbs like 'every day') and vocabulary to create a personal, creative product. The goal is task completion and expressing ideas clearly, even with minor errors.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[10-12 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Peer assessment using a simple checklist and subsequent summary writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students exchange their poems with a classmate. Each student receives a "Peer Feedback Form" with two sections: "What I liked" and "What can be improved."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read their partner's poem and use the form to write short, simple feedback. Examples for "What I liked": "Good job: used rhyming words!" "I like the words for chores." Examples for "What can be improved": "Needs work: add more lines about chores." "Maybe add another chore."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After giving feedback, each student writes a 2-3 sentence summary of the feedback they received on their own poem. Example: "Maria liked my poem. She said it was fun. She thinks I can add more chore words."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation & Peer Feedback Review):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Ideas:</b> Does the poem clearly talk about chores?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Does the poem include vocabulary words for chores (e.g., clean, wash, sweep, dishes, floor)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar (Present Simple):</b> Are the verbs used in the correct present simple form (e.g., "I clean," "He washes")?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Rhyming Pattern:</b> Does the poem attempt a simple rhyme?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Summary of Feedback:</b> Can the student write a simple, accurate summary of their peer's feedback?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write one sentence answering each question:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today in my poem is: ___."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The part of writing the poem that was easy/difficult was: ___."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[8-10 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I learn about writing poems today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I make my chore poem better next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., Students were engaged in peer feedback, most students could write a simple poem, target vocabulary was used.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Provide more visual cues for rhyming words, review present simple 'he/she/it' forms, give sentence frames for summarizing feedback.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the pronunciation points (/w/ and /s/) adequately addressed? Did students show recognition of closed and open syllables?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson focused on producing a written poem and summarizing peer feedback. The next lesson (Mediation & Integration) will likely involve revising these poems based on feedback, preparing them for a presentation, or integrating them into a larger project about daily routines and chores.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[4-5 min]</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>